Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 06:56:45 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 254/307] !B (Terrain Plate) Cull plate triangles buried below
 terrain.

Move triangles whose vertices are all safely below terrain into a no-draw
subset while generating the plate mesh. Buried surfaces and skirts remain
hidden even when coarser terrain LODs dip below the full-resolution ground.
---
 .../Geometry/TerrainPlateComponent.cpp        | 228 +++++++++++++++---
 .../Geometry/TerrainPlateComponent.h          |   4 +
 2 files changed, 201 insertions(+), 31 deletions(-)

diff --git a/Code/CryPlugins/CryDefaultEntities/Module/DefaultComponents/Geometry/TerrainPlateComponent.cpp b/Code/CryPlugins/CryDefaultEntities/Module/DefaultComponents/Geometry/TerrainPlateComponent.cpp
index cf6db949..7be72846 100644
--- a/Code/CryPlugins/CryDefaultEntities/Module/DefaultComponents/Geometry/TerrainPlateComponent.cpp
+++ b/Code/CryPlugins/CryDefaultEntities/Module/DefaultComponents/Geometry/TerrainPlateComponent.cpp
@@ -4,6 +4,7 @@
 
 #include <Cry3DEngine/I3DEngine.h>
 #include <Cry3DEngine/IIndexedMesh.h>
+#include <Cry3DEngine/IMaterial.h>   // MTL_FLAG_NODRAW, the buried subset marker
 #include <Cry3DEngine/IStatObj.h>
 #include <Cry3DEngine/IRenderNode.h>
 #include <Cry3DEngine/ImageExtensionHelper.h>
@@ -206,6 +207,42 @@ const uint32 kSunkenPollFrames = 16;
 //! terrain itself and could no longer promise it had seen every bump, so it refuses to hide instead.
 const int kSunkenMaxSamplesPerAxis = 257;
 
+//! P18, 2026-08-31, screenshot verified by the user with a red debug material: the band that pokes
+//! through the ground at distance is NOT "the plate". It is the plate's BELOW GROUND surface regions and
+//! its SKIRT - the parts that the terrain integration classifies as rejected (the height band test
+//! triBox.max.z > fElevMin, terrain_sector_render.cpp) and which therefore wear the plate's own fallback
+//! material instead of the terrain shading. They are meant to be invisible because they are buried, and
+//! decision 02 says so in as many words: "sunk -> the terrain wins the depth test". As P17 established,
+//! that promise only holds for the surface the depth test actually rasterises, and a terrain sector at
+//! LOD >= 1 draws the chord between two sampled heightmap nodes, dipping locally below the real ground by
+//! up to m_geomError. Wherever it dips, the buried geometry is revealed.
+//! P17 half A hides the WHOLE plate, and it never fires in practice, because a real plate is a mound with
+//! buried edges: partially sunken, so one sample above ground vetoes the hide. C4 only pushes the reveal
+//! distance out. The thing that has to go is therefore not the plate but the buried triangles.
+//! So: during mesh generation, classify every triangle against the terrain the weld already samples, and
+//! move a triangle whose three vertices are ALL below that terrain into a second, MTL_FLAG_NODRAW subset.
+//! A NODRAW subset is the engine's own idiom for "geometry that exists but is not drawn":
+//!  - CRenderMesh::SetMesh skips it when it builds render chunks (RenderMesh.cpp:1182, :1196, :1268), so
+//!    it never reaches any render pass, near or far, camera or shadow;
+//!  - CStatObj::PhysicalizeGeomType selects subsets by nPhysicalizeType alone (StatObjPhys.cpp:155-174),
+//!    so the collision trimesh still contains every triangle: the physics proxy does not change;
+//!  - CTerrainNode::AppendTrianglesFromObjects walks render chunks and skips NODRAW ones
+//!    (terrain_sector_render.cpp:1624), so the buried triangles leave the terrain integration copy too -
+//!    which changes nothing, because the height band test there rejected them anyway.
+//! No vertex is moved, removed or renumbered: only the ORDER of the index buffer changes, so the weld
+//! ring still welds, the UVs and the tangent frame of every surviving triangle are untouched, and the
+//! bake - which samples the plate's SOURCE height field through GetPlateReliefAt, never the render mesh -
+//! cannot see this at all.
+//! Escape hatch: e_TerrainPlateCullBuried 0 keeps every triangle in the drawn subset (stock behaviour).
+int    g_terrainPlateCullBuried = 1;
+ICVar* g_pTerrainPlateCullBuriedCVar = nullptr;
+
+//! Metres a vertex has to be under the terrain before it counts as buried. The same 0.05 m as
+//! kSunkenHideClearance, and for the same reason: it is half the default Weld Sink (0.1 m), so the welded
+//! rim - which is pushed under the ground ON PURPOSE - is correctly seen as buried, while float noise
+//! cannot flip a vertex that is meant to sit on the surface.
+const float kBuriedCullMargin = 0.05f;
+
 //! Bake trigger / bake apply tracing. The bake chain has a dozen places where it can legitimately do
 //! nothing (the property is off, the level's terrain is not in yet, the footprint is empty, the write
 //! was refused), and until this cvar existed every one of them was a silent return: the user ticked
@@ -337,6 +374,27 @@ void RegisterTerrainPlateCVars()
 		g_pTerrainPlateHideSunkenCVar->AddOnChange(&CTerrainPlateComponent::OnHideSunkenCVarChanged);
 	}
 
+	g_pTerrainPlateCullBuriedCVar = REGISTER_CVAR2("e_TerrainPlateCullBuried", &g_terrainPlateCullBuried, 1, VF_NULL,
+	                                              "Terrain Plate component: leave the plate's buried triangles out of the DRAWN geometry. A\n"
+	                                              "triangle is buried when all three of its vertices are more than 5 cm below the terrain\n"
+	                                              "surface under them - the surface regions of a partially sunken plate and, with the default\n"
+	                                              "Weld Sink, the whole skirt. Those parts are rejected by the terrain integration and so\n"
+	                                              "wear the plate's own fallback material; they are meant to be hidden by the ground, and near\n"
+	                                              "the camera they are, but a terrain sector at LOD 1 or coarser draws the chord between two\n"
+	                                              "heightmap nodes and dips locally below the real ground, which is what reveals them as a\n"
+	                                              "scalloped band at distance.\n"
+	                                              "Collision is NOT affected: the buried triangles stay in the physics trimesh, they are only\n"
+	                                              "moved into a MTL_FLAG_NODRAW subset. Re-evaluated on every mesh rebuild.\n"
+	                                              "1 - default\n"
+	                                              "0 - stock behaviour: every triangle is drawn");
+
+	// The classification is baked into the index buffer, so the cvar can only take effect by rebuilding
+	// every plate's mesh - unlike e_TerrainPlateHideSunken, which is a render flag.
+	if (g_pTerrainPlateCullBuriedCVar != nullptr)
+	{
+		g_pTerrainPlateCullBuriedCVar->AddOnChange(&CTerrainPlateComponent::OnCullBuriedCVarChanged);
+	}
+
 	g_pTerrainPlateDebugCVar = REGISTER_CVAR2("e_TerrainPlateDebug", &g_terrainPlateDebug, 0, VF_NULL,
 	                                          "Terrain Plate component: trace the bake trigger and the bake itself.\n"
 	                                          "0 - off\n"
@@ -376,6 +434,9 @@ void UnregisterTerrainPlateCVars()
 		if (g_pTerrainPlateHideSunkenCVar != nullptr)
 			gEnv->pConsole->UnregisterVariable(g_pTerrainPlateHideSunkenCVar->GetName());
 
+		if (g_pTerrainPlateCullBuriedCVar != nullptr)
+			gEnv->pConsole->UnregisterVariable(g_pTerrainPlateCullBuriedCVar->GetName());
+
 		if (g_pTerrainPlateDebugCVar != nullptr)
 			gEnv->pConsole->UnregisterVariable(g_pTerrainPlateDebugCVar->GetName());
 	}
@@ -388,6 +449,7 @@ void UnregisterTerrainPlateCVars()
 	g_pTerrainPlateBakeEdgeAACVar = nullptr;
 	g_pTerrainPlateBakeWeldCurveCVar = nullptr;
 	g_pTerrainPlateHideSunkenCVar = nullptr;
+	g_pTerrainPlateCullBuriedCVar = nullptr;
 	g_pTerrainPlateDebugCVar = nullptr;
 }
 
@@ -1865,28 +1927,6 @@ void CTerrainPlateComponent::RegenerateMesh()
 		}
 	}
 
-	// Grid indices, counter clockwise seen from +Z (the engine's front face winding, see
-	// CTerrain::MakeAreaRenderMesh).
-	int indexCursor = 0;
-	for (int r = 0; r < ny - 1; ++r)
-	{
-		for (int c = 0; c < nx - 1; ++c)
-		{
-			const vtx_idx v00 = (vtx_idx)(r * nx + c);
-			const vtx_idx v01 = (vtx_idx)(r * nx + c + 1);
-			const vtx_idx v10 = (vtx_idx)((r + 1) * nx + c);
-			const vtx_idx v11 = (vtx_idx)((r + 1) * nx + c + 1);
-
-			pIndices[indexCursor++] = v00;
-			pIndices[indexCursor++] = v10;
-			pIndices[indexCursor++] = v01;
-
-			pIndices[indexCursor++] = v01;
-			pIndices[indexCursor++] = v10;
-			pIndices[indexCursor++] = v11;
-		}
-	}
-
 	// Skirt: a duplicated border ring plus a second ring pushed straight down, closed with quads whose
 	// normals face outwards. The duplication is what lets the side wall have its own horizontal normal.
 	// The skirt starts at the welded (and sunk) ring, so with the default Weld Sink it is entirely below
@@ -1932,21 +1972,98 @@ void CTerrainPlateComponent::RegenerateMesh()
 			pTangents[topBase + i] = SMeshTangents(along, down, outward);
 			pTangents[bottomBase + i] = SMeshTangents(along, down, outward);
 		}
+	}
+
+	// ---------------------------------------------------------------- buried triangle classification
+	// P18. Every vertex position is final by now (weld included), so one pass over the position stream
+	// decides, per vertex, whether it is under the ground. @see the e_TerrainPlateCullBuried comment at
+	// the top of this file for what this is for and why a NODRAW subset is the mechanism.
+	//
+	// The terrain reference is I3DEngine::GetTerrainElevation (CTerrain::GetZApr), deliberately the very
+	// same sampler the edge weld above uses. That makes the test the exact inverse of the weld's own
+	// intent: the weld pushes the outer ring Weld Sink metres BELOW this surface so that no light leaks
+	// under the border, and this test then reads those vertices as buried, which is what they are. Using a
+	// different terrain estimate here (the per quad minimum of P17's whole plate test, say) would judge
+	// the ring against a surface the weld never saw and would keep the rim on slopes, where the artefact
+	// is worst. Off the terrain GetZApr returns TERRAIN_BOTTOM_LEVEL, so nothing is ever culled there -
+	// the safe direction.
+	std::vector<char> buried;
+	const bool bCullBuried = (g_terrainPlateCullBuried != 0) && (gEnv->p3DEngine->GetITerrain() != nullptr);
+
+	if (bCullBuried)
+	{
+		const Matrix34 worldTM = GetPlateWorldTM();
+		buried.resize((size_t)totalVertexCount, 0);
+
+		for (int i = 0; i < totalVertexCount; ++i)
+		{
+			const Vec3 world = worldTM * pPositions[i];
+			const float terrainZ = gEnv->p3DEngine->GetTerrainElevation(world.x, world.y);
+			buried[(size_t)i] = (world.z < terrainZ - kBuriedCullMargin) ? 1 : 0;
+		}
+	}
+
+	// Culled triangles are not thrown away, they are moved to the END of the index buffer, where a second
+	// subset marked MTL_FLAG_NODRAW picks them up: the render mesh drops them, the physics trimesh keeps
+	// them. The buffer keeps its full totalIndexCount and no vertex is touched.
+	int indexCursor = 0;
+	std::vector<vtx_idx> culledIndices;
+
+	auto emitTriangle = [&](int a, int b, int c)
+	{
+		if (bCullBuried && buried[(size_t)a] && buried[(size_t)b] && buried[(size_t)c])
+		{
+			culledIndices.push_back((vtx_idx)a);
+			culledIndices.push_back((vtx_idx)b);
+			culledIndices.push_back((vtx_idx)c);
+			return;
+		}
+
+		pIndices[indexCursor++] = (vtx_idx)a;
+		pIndices[indexCursor++] = (vtx_idx)b;
+		pIndices[indexCursor++] = (vtx_idx)c;
+	};
+
+	// Grid indices, counter clockwise seen from +Z (the engine's front face winding, see
+	// CTerrain::MakeAreaRenderMesh).
+	for (int r = 0; r < ny - 1; ++r)
+	{
+		for (int c = 0; c < nx - 1; ++c)
+		{
+			const int v00 = r * nx + c;
+			const int v01 = r * nx + c + 1;
+			const int v10 = (r + 1) * nx + c;
+			const int v11 = (r + 1) * nx + c + 1;
+
+			emitTriangle(v00, v10, v01);
+			emitTriangle(v01, v10, v11);
+		}
+	}
+
+	// Skirt indices. The same rule applies and it is the rule the skirt was written for: the skirt exists
+	// to seal the border where the Weld Sink alone is not enough, and a skirt quad whose TOP edge is
+	// already under the ground seals nothing - it is only something to be revealed by a dipping LOD.
+	if (bSkirt)
+	{
+		const int topBase = gridVertexCount;
+		const int bottomBase = gridVertexCount + perimeterCount;
 
 		for (int i = 0; i < perimeterCount; ++i)
 		{
 			const int j = (i + 1) % perimeterCount;
 
-			pIndices[indexCursor++] = (vtx_idx)(topBase + i);
-			pIndices[indexCursor++] = (vtx_idx)(bottomBase + i);
-			pIndices[indexCursor++] = (vtx_idx)(bottomBase + j);
-
-			pIndices[indexCursor++] = (vtx_idx)(topBase + i);
-			pIndices[indexCursor++] = (vtx_idx)(bottomBase + j);
-			pIndices[indexCursor++] = (vtx_idx)(topBase + j);
+			emitTriangle(topBase + i, bottomBase + i, bottomBase + j);
+			emitTriangle(topBase + i, bottomBase + j, topBase + j);
 		}
 	}
 
+	const int visibleIndexCount = indexCursor;
+
+	for (vtx_idx culled : culledIndices)
+	{
+		pIndices[indexCursor++] = culled;
+	}
+
 	CRY_ASSERT(indexCursor == totalIndexCount);
 
 	// The hash is what tells a real geometry change from a rebuild that produced the same mesh (flipping
@@ -1966,11 +2083,42 @@ void CTerrainPlateComponent::RegenerateMesh()
 		}
 	}
 
-	pIndexedMesh->SetSubSetCount(1);
-	pIndexedMesh->SetSubsetIndexVertexRanges(0, 0, totalIndexCount, 0, totalVertexCount);
+	// The burial split is part of what the shadow cache holds, and it can change without a single vertex
+	// moving: a plate with Edge Weld off keeps its exact geometry while the ground under it is sculpted.
+	// Folding the visible count in is what makes InvalidateShadowCache fire in that case.
+	geometryHash = (geometryHash ^ (uint32)visibleIndexCount) * 16777619u;
+
+	// Two subsets over one index buffer: [0, visibleIndexCount) is drawn and physicalised, the rest is
+	// physicalised only. Both keep nMatID 0 and PHYS_GEOM_TYPE_DEFAULT, so CStatObj::PhysicalizeGeomType
+	// (StatObjPhys.cpp:155-174, which selects on nPhysicalizeType and never looks at nMatFlags) builds the
+	// collision trimesh from every triangle, exactly as it did with one subset - the physics proxy is
+	// unchanged by this feature in every configuration. The vertex range is the whole stream for both,
+	// because the split is per triangle and the two share vertices along their boundary.
+	const int culledIndexCount = totalIndexCount - visibleIndexCount;
+
+	pIndexedMesh->SetSubSetCount(culledIndexCount > 0 ? 2 : 1);
+	pIndexedMesh->SetSubsetIndexVertexRanges(0, 0, visibleIndexCount, 0, totalVertexCount);
 	pIndexedMesh->SetSubsetMaterialId(0, 0);
 	pIndexedMesh->SetSubsetMaterialProperties(0, 0, PHYS_GEOM_TYPE_DEFAULT);
 	pIndexedMesh->SetSubsetBounds(0, bbox.GetCenter(), bbox.GetRadius());
+
+	if (culledIndexCount > 0)
+	{
+		pIndexedMesh->SetSubsetIndexVertexRanges(1, visibleIndexCount, culledIndexCount, 0, totalVertexCount);
+		pIndexedMesh->SetSubsetMaterialId(1, 0);
+		pIndexedMesh->SetSubsetMaterialProperties(1, MTL_FLAG_NODRAW, PHYS_GEOM_TYPE_DEFAULT);
+		pIndexedMesh->SetSubsetBounds(1, bbox.GetCenter(), bbox.GetRadius());
+
+		if (GetPlateDebugLevel() >= 1)
+		{
+			CryLog("TerrainPlate '%s': %d of %d triangles are buried and are not drawn (e_TerrainPlateCullBuried)",
+			       GetPlateName(), culledIndexCount / 3, totalIndexCount / 3);
+		}
+	}
+
+	// The bounding box is deliberately the WHOLE mesh, culled triangles included: it is what the physics
+	// proxy, the entity slot and the terrain sector invalidation are sized from, and shrinking it to the
+	// drawn part would leave the collision outside the box it is registered with.
 	pIndexedMesh->SetBBox(bbox);
 
 	const bool bPhysicalize = ((uint32)m_type & (uint32)EMeshType::Collider) != 0;
@@ -3461,6 +3609,24 @@ void CTerrainPlateComponent::OnHideSunkenCVarChanged(ICVar* /*pCVar*/)
 	}
 }
 
+void CTerrainPlateComponent::OnCullBuriedCVarChanged(ICVar* /*pCVar*/)
+{
+	// Unlike the hide flag, this one is not a render node flag that can simply be re-written: the split is
+	// in the index buffer, so the only way to honour the new value is to build the mesh again.
+	// RequestRegenerate rather than RegenerateMesh, so that toggling the cvar while several plates share
+	// ground coalesces into one rebuild per plate per frame and the bake cascade runs from the update tick
+	// like every other rebuild does.
+	//
+	// The vector is iterated by index: RegenerateMesh can start a bake cascade, which regenerates other
+	// plates, and a plate is only ever appended to or erased from g_terrainPlates by Initialize / teardown
+	// - neither of which a rebuild triggers - but an index walk survives a reallocation that a range-for
+	// would not.
+	for (size_t i = 0; i < g_terrainPlates.size(); ++i)
+	{
+		g_terrainPlates[i]->RequestRegenerate();
+	}
+}
+
 bool CTerrainPlateComponent::WantsSunkenHideTick() const
 {
 	// A visible plate costs nothing per frame: it is re-tested by the events that can change the answer
diff --git a/Code/CryPlugins/CryDefaultEntities/Module/DefaultComponents/Geometry/TerrainPlateComponent.h b/Code/CryPlugins/CryDefaultEntities/Module/DefaultComponents/Geometry/TerrainPlateComponent.h
index ecb6ec52..87b4c20a 100644
--- a/Code/CryPlugins/CryDefaultEntities/Module/DefaultComponents/Geometry/TerrainPlateComponent.h
+++ b/Code/CryPlugins/CryDefaultEntities/Module/DefaultComponents/Geometry/TerrainPlateComponent.h
@@ -163,6 +163,10 @@ public:
 	//! Public only because RegisterTerrainPlateCVars hands it to ICVar::AddOnChange as a plain function.
 	static void OnHideSunkenCVarChanged(ICVar* pCVar);
 
+	//! e_TerrainPlateCullBuried changed: the classification lives in the index buffer, so every plate in
+	//! the level has to rebuild its mesh for the cvar to mean anything. Public for the same reason.
+	static void OnCullBuriedCVarChanged(ICVar* pCVar);
+
 protected:
 	friend CPlugin_CryDefaultEntities;
 	static void Register(Schematyc::CEnvRegistrationScope& componentScope);
